Lazy Expiration vs Active Expiration

Lazy Expiration

- Triggered when a client accesses a key.
- Redis checks whether the key has expired only during commands like GET, EXISTS, etc.
- If expired:
  - Delete the key.
  - Return nil (or key not found).
- If the key is never accessed again, it can remain in memory.

Example:

SET token abc
EXPIRE token 10

(15 seconds later)

GET token

↓

Redis notices it expired

↓

Deletes it

↓

Returns nil

------------------------------------------------------------

Active Expiration

- Triggered automatically by Redis in the background.
- Redis periodically wakes up and checks a small random sample of keys that have expiry times.
- If a key has expired:
  - Delete it immediately.
- Works even if no client accesses the key.

Example:

SET otp 123456
EXPIRE otp 300

(No client ever accesses the key)

↓

Redis background task runs

↓

Finds otp has expired

↓

Deletes it automatically

------------------------------------------------------------

Lazy Expiration
- Runs on client access.
- Deletes keys only when they are used.
- Saves CPU.
- Expired keys may stay in memory if never accessed.

Active Expiration
- Runs periodically in the background.
- Deletes expired keys even if nobody accesses them.
- Frees memory automatically.
- Uses a small amount of CPU for cleanup.

Redis uses both Lazy Expiration and Active Expiration together to balance CPU usage and memory usage.